  "body": {
    "id": "thm:regular-efficiency",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Along every sequence in \\(\\mathcal M_N\\) with \\(\\inf_N\\mu_D>0\\), the profiled Anderson--Rubin interval is first-order equivalent to the Wald interval based on the minimum-variance scalar residual-score influence representation, whose variance is \\(V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P,\\theta_T)/\\mu_D^2\\). When \\(K=1\\), this influence function is the canonical gradient \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\) in the nonparametric observed-law model, so the profiled interval is semiparametrically efficient and recovers the single-source result. For \\(K\\ge2\\), the equivalence is only to the scalar residual-score Wald interval; for allowed class constants, separate outcome and receipt transport can make the unrestricted efficiency bound strictly smaller.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "Mbase",
      "N",
      "muD",
      "thetaT",
