    "justification": "The identification-robust procedure remains efficient under strong compliance.",
    "gap": "Rudolph and van der Laan derive a single-source ratio gradient, while Su et al. derive multi-source weights for a nonratio effect; neither gives the joint residual allocation.",
    "consumer": "Regular multi-site analyses sacrifice no first-order precision by using robust inversion.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(a^{\\star}(\\theta_T)\\) be the unique minimizer of the scalar allocation program and let \\(\\Phi_{a^{\\star}}\\) be the product-experiment residual influence score constructed in the pooled-information proof. Along a regular path with derivatives \\((\\dot\\mu_Y,\\dot\\mu_D)\\), quotient algebra is justified by \\(\\inf_N\\mu_D>0\\) and gives\n\\[\n \\dot\\theta_T\n =\\frac{\\dot\\mu_Y-\\theta_T\\dot\\mu_D}{\\mu_D}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n \\phi_{\\mathrm{sc}}=\\frac{\\Phi_{a^{\\star}}}{\\mu_D}\n\\]\nis a valid regular influence function and\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Var}_P(\\phi_{\\mathrm{sc}})\n =\\frac{V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P,\\theta_T)}{\\mu_D^2}.\n\\]\nThe pooled-information theorem proves minimality within the scalar residual-score class. When \\(K=1\\), its final argument shows that componentwise transport forces the same sole coefficient vector, so \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{sc}}=\\phi_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\) and the displayed variance is the nonparametric observed-law efficiency bound.\n\nUniform consistency of the profiler and the score expansion in the honesty proof imply, on every sequence with compliance bounded away from zero,\n\\[\n \\widehat m_N(\\theta)\n =\\widehat m_N(\\theta_T)-\\mu_D(\\theta-\\theta_T)+o_P(N^{-1/2})\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(|\\theta-\\theta_T|=O(N^{-1/2})\\), and\n\\[\n \\widehat V_N(\\theta)\n =V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P,\\theta_T)+o_P(N^{-1}).\n\\]\nWriting \\(z_{1-\\alpha/2}=\\sqrt{\\chi^2_{1,1-\\alpha}}\\), the two local roots of the Anderson--Rubin acceptance inequality are therefore\n\\[\n \\theta_T+\\frac{\\widehat m_N(\\theta_T)}{\\mu_D}\n \\mathbin{\\pm}\n z_{1-\\alpha/2}\n \\frac{\\sqrt{V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P,\\theta_T)}}{\\mu_D}\n +o_P(N^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nThese are precisely the Wald endpoints based on \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{sc}}\\), to first order. If the fixed boundary of \\([-1,1]\\) is active, both intervals undergo the same first-order truncation; taking the hull does not change the local endpoint expansion. This proves scalar-Wald equivalence for every \\(K\\) and semiparametric efficiency for \\(K=1\\). The explicit two-source counterexample in the pooled-information proof shows why no unrestricted multi-source efficiency conclusion follows without an additional covariance compatibility condition.",
